tel 1.0

base obligatory
import soundness

info   typed remote procedure calls: JSON-RPC, gRPC, server-sent events and message framing
detail
    _Obligatory_ makes a remote procedure call typed at both ends. An RPC interface is an
    ordinary trait whose methods carry the `@rpc` annotation; from that one definition, a
    macro derives a **server-side dispatcher** which routes incoming requests to an
    implementation, and a **client stub** which turns each method call into a wire message, so
    the two sides cannot disagree about a method's name, its parameters or its result. The
    same shape serves two protocols: JSON-RPC 2.0, whose payloads are `Json`, and gRPC over
    HTTP/2, whose payloads are Protocol Buffers messages.

    Beneath the protocols sits the plumbing of message streams. **Framing** carves a text or
    byte stream into its messages by a convention named as a type — `LengthPrefix`,
    `ContentLength`, `CrLf`, `Linefeed`, `CarriageReturn`, `Sse` or `Grpc.Framing` — through
    the `frames` extension and the `Framable` typeclass; and an `Sse` value is one server-sent
    event, with `Sse.Source` holding a replayable buffer of them for a `text/event-stream`
    response.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Ping(message: Text)
    case class Pong(message: Text)

    trait Echo:
      @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong

    object EchoService extends Echo:
      def call(request: Ping): Pong = Pong(request.message)

    val dispatch = JsonRpc.serve[Echo](EchoService)
    val request: Json = t"""{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"call","params":{"request":{"message":"hi"}},"id":1}""".read[Json]
    dispatch(request)   // the response document, {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":{"message":"hi"},"id":1}
    ```

    A method whose result type is `Unit` is a *notification*: the client sends it without an
    id and awaits no response, and the dispatcher answers it with `Unset`. Every other method
    is a request, correlated by id. On the calling side, `remote[Echo](url)` manufactures a
    client which POSTs each call to an HTTP URL as JSON-RPC, and a `JsonRpc` instance's
    `client`/`proxy` manufactures one over a bidirectional channel of `Json` messages, which is
    how a language-server (`exegesis`), an agent (`espionage`) or an MCP session
    (`synesthesia`) talks to its peer. For gRPC, `Grpc.remote[Echo](channel, t"echo.Echo")`
    derives the stub over a `Grpc.Channel`, which is a single persistent HTTP/2 connection: a
    method returning a value makes a unary call, one returning a `Chain` a server-streaming
    call, and a non-`Ok` status arrives as a `Grpc.Error` carrying the canonical
    `Grpc.Status`.

    MCP builds on the JSON-RPC half: `Mcp.Server` in `synesthesia` is a `JsonRpc` whose
    `Origin` is the client's interface, every request is dispatched by `JsonRpc.serve`, faults
    are answered with `JsonRpc.failure`, and the server-to-client half of MCP's streamable HTTP
    transport is the `JsonRpc#stream` of `Sse` events, served as `text/event-stream` by
    `Sse.servable`. The Language Server Protocol does the same over `ContentLength`-framed
    standard streams, and the Agent Client Protocol over `Linefeed`-framed ones.

    The library is split into three source sets:

    - `obligatory.core` — the framing typeclass `Framable`, the `frames` extension, the
      framers `CarriageReturn`, `Linefeed`, `CrLf`, `ContentLength` and `LengthPrefix`,
      `Framing.Error`, the `@rpc` and `@bare` annotations and `Rpc.Error`. It compiles for the
      JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `obligatory.json` — `JsonRpc`, `remote` and `Sse`. JVM-only, as it reaches HTTP through
      `telekinesis` and `jacinta.http`.
    - `obligatory.grpc` — `Grpc` and `GrpcSessional`, over `telekinesis`'s HTTP/2 client and
      `locomotion`'s Protocol Buffers. JVM-only.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The library has no choice packages:
    every given lives in the companion of its subject (`Sse.framable`, `LengthPrefix.framable`,
    `Grpc.Endpoint.sessional`, …) and is found through implicit scope with no import. What a
    sample does need is a `Tactic` for the errors a framer or a call raises — `import
    strategies.throwUnsafely` at the least — and, for anything over a connection, a
    `supervise` scope supplying a `Monitor` and a `Probate`.

refer obligatory.JsonRpc
refer obligatory.Sse
refer obligatory.Grpc
refer obligatory.Framable
refer obligatory.frames
refer obligatory.remote

entry .Framable   the typeclass which carves a stream into frames by a named convention
  detail
      `element is Framable by frame` is the evidence that a stream of `element`s (`Text` or
      `Data`) can be split into messages by the convention `frame` names — `CrLf`, `Linefeed`,
      `CarriageReturn`, `ContentLength`, `LengthPrefix`, `Sse` or `Grpc.Framing` — each a
      sealed, uninhabited type whose companion holds the given. The `frames` extension on an
      `Iterator[element]^` or a `Chain[element]` applies it: `chain.frames[Linefeed]`.

      A framer keeps its own position in a `Cursor` over the input, so chunk boundaries mean
      nothing to it: a message split across three chunks, two messages in one chunk, and a
      final message with no terminator are all delivered whole.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Chain(t"one\ntwo\nth", t"ree").frames[Linefeed].to[List]   // List(t"one", t"two", t"three")
      ```

      The framed iterator pulls lazily from its input and so captures it, and captures the
      framer too, since a framer built from a `Tactic[Framing.Error]` raises through it during
      iteration rather than up front. A framer which needs a tactic (`CrLf`, `ContentLength`,
      `LengthPrefix`, `Grpc.Framing`) therefore needs one in scope where `frames` is called;
      `CarriageReturn`, `Linefeed` and `Sse` cannot fail and need none.

      A user-defined framer is a `new Framable` with `Self` and `Operand` set and `frames`
      implemented, most easily over `Framable.frames` and a `Cursor`.
  refer obligatory.frames
  refer obligatory.Framing
  entry .frames   wraps a thunk which reads one frame, or `Unset` at the end, as an iterator
      `Framable.frames[data](frame)` turns a by-name expression — which reads and returns the
      next frame, or `Unset` once the input is exhausted — into a `murmuration.Iterator[data]`,
      hiding the one-ahead buffering every framer would otherwise duplicate. The thunk
      typically closes over the caller's `Cursor`, so the iterator captures the thunk
      (`^{frame, caps.any}`). This is how every framer in the library, and `Sse.framable`, is
      written.
  entry #frames   the framed messages of an input stream, as a lazy one-shot iterator
      Takes an `Iterator[Self]^` and returns the `Iterator[Self]^{input, this}` of its frames.
      Called through the `frames` extension rather than directly.

entry .frames   `stream.frames[Convention]`: the messages of a stream, by a framing convention
    An extension on an `Iterator[element]^` and on a `Chain[element]`, each returning the
    one-shot `Iterator[element]` of messages the `element is Framable by frame` given in scope
    delimits. The type argument names the convention: `frames[LengthPrefix]`,
    `frames[ContentLength]`, `frames[CrLf]`, `frames[Linefeed]`, `frames[CarriageReturn]`,
    `frames[Sse]` or `frames[Grpc.Framing]`. The receiver is consumed: a framed iterator is the
    only reader of its input.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val prefixed = Chain(Data(0, 0, 0, 2, 104, 105))
    val headed = Chain(t"Content-Length: 2\r\n\r\nhi".in[Data])
    val lines = Chain(t"one\r\ntwo\r\n")

    prefixed.frames[LengthPrefix].to[List]     // one frame, the bytes of "hi"
    headed.frames[ContentLength].to[List]      // one frame, byte-counted: "hi"
    lines.frames[CrLf].to[List]                // List(t"one", t"two")
    lines.frames[Linefeed].to[List]            // List(t"one\r", t"two\r")
    ```

    The evidence is capture-polymorphic: a framer which raises `Framing.Error` (`CrLf`,
    `ContentLength`, `LengthPrefix`) needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]` in scope at the call, and
    the iterator retains it, raising a truncated or malformed frame as it is read rather than
    when `frames` is called. A `Chain` receiver is how a stream's `.chain` arrives:
    `stream.chain.frames[Linefeed]` reads a subprocess's output line by line.

entry .Framing   the act of delimiting messages in a stream, and the error when it fails
  detail
      `Framing` is the home of `Framing.Error`, the error every framer raises on a stream it
      cannot deframe. It is named for the act rather than the frame — obligatory has no `Frame`
      type — and is distinct from `Grpc.Framing`, the gRPC message convention, which raises
      `Grpc.Error` instead.
  refer obligatory.Framable
  entry .Error   raised when a stream cannot be split into frames: error 142
    detail
        Error 142: _could not deframe the message because `reason`_. Raised, through the
        `Tactic[Framing.Error]` the framer's given captured, as the framed iterator is read.
        The `reason` says what was wrong: `ShortRead` when the input ended inside a frame
        (`LengthPrefix` with fewer bytes than its header promised, `ContentLength` with a
        truncated header or body); `MissingLineFeed` when `CrLf` met a carriage return not
        followed by a line feed; `MalformedLength` when a `Content-Length` header's value was
        absent or not a number; and `UnknownHeader`, reserved for a header the protocol
        rejects (`ContentLength` currently skips unrecognised headers rather than raising it).
    entry .Reason   why a frame could not be read
        `ShortRead` (1), _the input ended before a complete frame could be read_;
        `MissingLineFeed` (2), _a carriage return was not followed by a line feed_;
        `MalformedLength` (3), _the content-length header value could not be parsed_;
        `UnknownHeader` (4), _an unrecognized header was encountered_. The number is the
        error's detail number.
    entry .communicable   renders each `Framing.Error.Reason` as its message
    entry #reason   why the frame could not be read

entry .CarriageReturn   the framing convention of lines ended by a bare carriage return
  detail
      A sealed type naming the convention `frames[CarriageReturn]` applies to a `Text` stream:
      each frame is the text before a `\r`, which is consumed; a final fragment with no
      terminator is yielded as it stands, and an empty input yields nothing. The convention
      cannot fail, so no tactic is needed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Chain(t"one\rtwo\r", t"three").frames[CarriageReturn].to[List]   // List(t"one", t"two", t"three")
      ```
  entry .framable   the `Text is Framable by CarriageReturn` given, in implicit scope

entry .Linefeed   the framing convention of lines ended by a line feed, for text or bytes
  detail
      A sealed type naming the convention `frames[Linefeed]` applies: each frame is the
      content before a `\n`, which is consumed; a final unterminated fragment is yielded as it
      stands, and empty lines are retained as empty frames. It cannot fail, so no tactic is
      needed. Two givens serve it: `.framable` for a `Text` stream, and `.framableData` for a
      `Data` stream, which splits on the raw byte before any text decoding, so multi-byte
      UTF-8 content passes through intact — the shape of newline-delimited JSON over a binary
      stream, as the Agent Client Protocol uses.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      Chain(t"one\ntwo\nth", t"ree").frames[Linefeed].to[List]   // List(t"one", t"two", t"three")

      Chain(t"""{"text":"café"}\n{"text":"naïve"}\n""".in[Data]).frames[Linefeed].map(_.utf8).to[List]
      ```

      The byte framer reads *live* streams, such as a subprocess's output: it consumes a
      terminator with the cursor's non-refilling `advance()`, so a line which ends flush with
      its chunk is delivered at once rather than blocking for bytes beyond it; the refill is
      paid at the head of the next line.
  entry .framable   the `Text is Framable by Linefeed` given, in implicit scope
  entry .framableData   the `Data is Framable by Linefeed` given: splits bytes on the raw `\n` byte
      In implicit scope. Splits on byte 10 before any decoding, so UTF-8 content is never cut
      inside a character; a final unterminated fragment is yielded as-is.

entry .CrLf   the framing convention of lines ended by a carriage return and line feed
  detail
      A sealed type naming the convention `frames[CrLf]` applies to a `Text` stream: each
      frame is the text before `\r\n`, both of which are consumed; a final fragment with no
      terminator is yielded as it stands. A `\r` which is not followed by `\n` is a protocol
      violation, raised as `Framing.Error(MissingLineFeed)`, so the given needs a
      `Tactic[Framing.Error]` in scope, which the framed iterator retains.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Chain(t"one\r\ntwo\r\nthree").frames[CrLf].to[List]   // List(t"one", t"two", t"three")
      ```
  entry .framable   the `Text is Framable by CrLf` given; needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`

entry .ContentLength   the framing convention of a `Content-Length` header block before each body
  detail
      A sealed type naming the convention `frames[ContentLength]` applies to a `Data` stream:
      the base protocol of the Language Server Protocol, and the HTTP-style framing it derives
      from. Each message is a block of `Name: value` headers, each ended by `\r\n`, a blank
      `\r\n`, then exactly `Content-Length` bytes of body, yielded as `Data`. The count is of
      *bytes*, as the specification defines it, which is why the framer works on `Data` and
      leaves text decoding to the caller: counting characters coincides with bytes only for
      ASCII and corrupts the stream on any multi-byte UTF-8 body. Header names are matched
      case-insensitively, and headers other than `Content-Length` (`Content-Type`, say) are
      skipped.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val input = t"Content-Type: x\r\nContent-Length: 5\r\n\r\n12345Content-Length: 3\r\n\r\nabc"
      Chain(input.in[Data]).frames[ContentLength].map(_.utf8).to[List]   // List(t"12345", t"abc")
      ```

      A header block with no numeric `Content-Length` raises `Framing.Error(MalformedLength)`,
      and an input which ends inside a header or a body raises `Framing.Error(ShortRead)`, so
      the given needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`, which the framed iterator retains.
  entry .framable   the `Data is Framable by ContentLength` given; needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`

entry .LengthPrefix   the framing convention of a four-byte big-endian length before each record
  detail
      A sealed type naming the convention `frames[LengthPrefix]` applies to a `Data` stream:
      each record is four bytes of big-endian length, then that many bytes, yielded as `Data`.
      `.encode` is the writing half, prefixing one record for the wire, so a framed stream can
      be produced as well as read; a `Duplex` carrying BinTEL messages is framed this way.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val records = List(Data(1, 2, 3), Data(), Data(-1, -2))
      Chain(records.map(LengthPrefix.encode)*).frames[LengthPrefix].to[List].map(_.to[List])
      ```

      An input which ends inside a header or a record raises `Framing.Error(ShortRead)`, so the
      given needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`, which the framed iterator retains. Lengths are
      read with each byte masked before it is shifted, so a record of 128 bytes or more, or
      longer than 32767, is framed correctly despite `Data`'s signed bytes.
  entry .encode   prefixes one record with its length as four big-endian bytes
      `LengthPrefix.encode(record)` is the `Data` of the four-byte header followed by the
      record: the inverse of `.framable`, and what a writer of a length-prefixed stream calls
      for each record.
  entry .framable   the `Data is Framable by LengthPrefix` given; needs a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`

entry .Associable   a typeclass pairing a session value with the response it governs
  detail
      `Self is Associable by Operand to Target`: `association(operand)` makes a session value
      from an operand, and `associate(session)(response)` attaches that session to a response
      of the `Target` type. It is a hook for a transport which must mark each response with the
      session it belongs to (as MCP marks a response with its session id header); the library
      declares the typeclass and no instance of it.
  entry #association   makes the `Self` session value from an `Operand`
  entry #associate   attaches a session to a `Target` response, returning the marked response

entry .Rpc   the home of `Rpc.Error`, the generic failure of a remote call
  detail
      The object holds only `Rpc.Error`. The protocol-specific failures are `JsonRpc.Error` and
      `Grpc.Error`, each carrying what its wire format reports; `Rpc.Error` says only that a
      call failed.
  entry .Error   raised when a remote procedure call fails: error 983
      Error 983: _the remote procedure call failed_. A generic failure with no detail, for a
      transport which cannot say more; `JsonRpc.Error` and `Grpc.Error` carry the peer's code
      and message.

entry .rpc   the `@rpc` annotation: marks a method of an interface as a remote procedure
    `@rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong` declares that `call` is a method the protocol exposes.
    `JsonRpc.serve`, `JsonRpc#proxy`, `remote` and `Grpc.remote` each read an interface's
    `@rpc` methods (including those it inherits) and ignore every other member. The method's
    name is the wire method name — a JSON-RPC name with a slash is written as a backquoted
    identifier, `` @rpc def `prompts/list`(…) `` — its parameter names are the members of a
    JSON-RPC `params` object, and a `Unit` result marks a notification, which expects no
    response. A gRPC `@rpc` method takes exactly one request message. The annotation is
    declared in the `annotations` object and exported.

entry .bare   the `@bare` annotation: a JSON-RPC parameter which is the whole `params` value
    Marks one parameter of an `@rpc` method as decoded from the entire `params` value, rather
    than from the like-named field of a `params` object; the client side sends it as the whole
    `params` likewise. For a wire protocol whose `params` is a bare object decoded straight
    into one type, such as the LSP's `*/resolve` family. A method may declare at most one
    `@bare` parameter: a second is a compile error in `JsonRpc#proxy`. The annotation is
    declared in the `annotations` object and exported.

entry .annotations   the home of the `@rpc` and `@bare` annotations
  detail
      The two annotations are declared here, rather than at the top level of the package, so
      that `rpc` does not share a classfile name with `Rpc` on a case-insensitive filesystem;
      both are exported to `obligatory` and the `soundness` umbrella, so `@rpc` and `@bare`
      are written without qualification.
  entry .rpc   marks a method of an interface as a remote procedure; see `obligatory.rpc`
  entry .bare   marks a parameter as the whole JSON-RPC `params` value; see `obligatory.bare`

entry .JsonRpc   JSON-RPC 2.0: a dispatcher and client stub derived from an `@rpc` interface, over HTTP or a channel
  detail
      `JsonRpc` is two things. As an **object**, it is the JSON-RPC 2.0 toolkit: `.serve`
      derives a dispatcher from an interface of `@rpc` methods, `.Request`, `.Response` and
      `.Failure` are the three message shapes, `.failure` writes a conformant error response,
      and `.call`, `.request`, `.notification`, `.outcome` and `.answer` are the
      correlation machinery a client is built from. As a **trait**, it is a bidirectional
      connection: a protocol class extends `JsonRpc`, names the peer's interface as its
      `Origin`, and gets `#client` (a stub of that interface whose calls are queued on the
      connection's outgoing channel), `#put` for messages of its own, and `#outgoing` or
      `#stream` for the transport to drain.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An interface of remote methods
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Ping(message: Text)
            case class Pong(message: Text)

            trait Echo:
              @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong
              @rpc def notify(request: Ping): Unit
      frame
        label An implementation becomes a dispatcher
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Ping(message: Text)
            case class Pong(message: Text)

            trait Echo:
              @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong
              @rpc def notify(request: Ping): Unit

            object EchoService extends Echo:
              def call(request: Ping): Pong = Pong(request.message)
              def notify(request: Ping): Unit = ()

            val dispatch: Json => Optional[Json] = JsonRpc.serve[Echo](EchoService)
      frame
        label A request is dispatched and answered
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Ping(message: Text)
            case class Pong(message: Text)

            trait Echo:
              @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong
              @rpc def notify(request: Ping): Unit

            object EchoService extends Echo:
              def call(request: Ping): Pong = Pong(request.message)
              def notify(request: Ping): Unit = ()

            val dispatch: Json => Optional[Json] = JsonRpc.serve[Echo](EchoService)

            val request = t"""{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"call","params":{"request":{"message":"hi"}},"id":1}""".read[Json]
            dispatch(request)   // {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":{"message":"hi"},"id":1}
      frame
        label A notification is dispatched and produces no response
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Ping(message: Text)
            case class Pong(message: Text)

            trait Echo:
              @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong
              @rpc def notify(request: Ping): Unit

            object EchoService extends Echo:
              def call(request: Ping): Pong = Pong(request.message)
              def notify(request: Ping): Unit = ()

            val dispatch: Json => Optional[Json] = JsonRpc.serve[Echo](EchoService)

            val request = t"""{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"call","params":{"request":{"message":"hi"}},"id":1}""".read[Json]
            dispatch(request)   // {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":{"message":"hi"},"id":1}

            val notification = t"""{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notify","params":{"request":{"message":"hi"}}}""".read[Json]
            dispatch(notification)   // Unset
      ##
      ```

      A method's parameters are the members of the request's `params` object, by name, each
      decoded with its type's `Json.Decodable`; a `@bare` parameter is the whole `params`
      value instead. The result is encoded with its type's `Encodable in Json` and wrapped in
      a `Response` echoing the request's id. A method returning `Unit` is a notification,
      answered with `Unset`. A request whose method the interface does not declare throws
      `JsonRpc.Error(UnknownMethod)`, and one whose parameters do not decode throws a
      `Json.Error`, each for the serving loop to catch and answer with `.failure` (`-32601`
      and `-32602` in the specification's vocabulary). A message with no `method` member is a
      response, which the dispatcher routes to `.answer` and returns `Unset` for, so one
      dispatcher can read a bidirectional stream.

      The dispatcher closes over the implementation alone and retains only durable
      capabilities, so it may be called from a task. Each expansion of `.serve` inlines a
      schema-carrying codec for every method, and a very large interface — the Language
      Server Protocol's — can exceed the JVM's per-class constant pool: split it across
      several traits, derive a dispatcher per trait, and route by `.methods`.

      **Over HTTP.** `remote[Echo](url)` is the client for a dispatcher mounted behind an HTTP
      route: each call POSTs a `Request` to the URL and decodes the `Response`. **Over a
      channel.** For a long-lived bidirectional connection — standard streams framed by
      `ContentLength` or `Linefeed`, a socket, or an MCP session — a class extends the
      `JsonRpc` trait, and the transport's writer drains `#outgoing` while its reader feeds
      each incoming document to the dispatcher. `synesthesia`'s `Mcp.Server` is such a class,
      with `Origin = Mcp.Client`: its `serve` dispatches every POSTed request through `.serve`,
      answers faults with `.failure`, and streams its `#stream` of `Sse` events back as
      `text/event-stream`.
  refer obligatory.remote
  refer obligatory.rpc
  refer obligatory.bare
  refer obligatory.Sse
  entry .serve   derives a dispatcher, `Json => Optional[Json]`, from an interface implementation
      `JsonRpc.serve[Interface](implementation)` is a macro which reads the `@rpc` methods of
      `Interface` and returns a function from a request document to its response document —
      `Unset` for a notification, or for a response routed to `.answer`. Its result type is
      `Json ->{caps.any.only[Durable]} Optional[Json]`: the dispatcher retains only durable
      capabilities and the implementation, so it may be held by a task.

      The macro needs an `Encodable in Json` for every method's result type at the expansion
      site — a missing one is a compile error naming the method — and summons each parameter's
      `Json.Decodable` inside the generated dispatcher under a per-call tactic, so a missing
      decoder is reported by the compiler's own "no given instance" message at the `serve`
      site. An unknown method is thrown as `JsonRpc.Error(UnknownMethod)` and a parameter
      which does not decode as `Json.Error`, for the serving loop to catch and answer with
      `.failure`; no tactic is needed at the expansion site.
  entry .methods   the names of an interface's `@rpc` methods, for routing across several dispatchers
      `JsonRpc.methods[Interface]` is a `List[Text]` of the interface's `@rpc` method names,
      inherited ones included. A protocol too large for one dispatcher class derives one with
      `.serve` per sub-interface and routes each request to the dispatcher whose `methods`
      contain its `method`; `exegesis` does this for the LSP's seven groups of methods.
  entry .Request   a JSON-RPC request or notification document, as a value
    detail
        `Request(jsonrpc, method, params, id)`: the `jsonrpc` version (`"2.0"`), the `method`
        name, the `params` document, and the `id`, which is `Unset` for a notification. The
        companion's `.decodable` reads one from a `Json`, so `safely(json.as[JsonRpc.Request])`
        tells a request from a response — a response has no `method` member and fails to decode
        — and `json.as[JsonRpc.Request].id` is the id to echo in a failure.
    entry .decodable   the `JsonRpc.Request is Json.Decodable` given, in implicit scope
        A pure, throwing derivation anchor: it throws `Json.Error` rather than raising it
        through a scoped tactic, so it can be summoned inside `safely` without the summon
        capturing the tactic.
    entry #jsonrpc   the protocol version, `"2.0"`
    entry #method   the method name
    entry #params   the parameters: an object of named members, or a bare value for a `@bare` parameter
    entry #id   the correlation id, or `Unset` for a notification
  entry .Response   a successful JSON-RPC response document, as a value
    detail
        `Response(jsonrpc, result, id)`: the version, the encoded `result`, and the `id` of
        the request it answers. `JsonRpc.serve` builds one for each request it dispatches; a protocol
        which answers requests itself (a proxy, say) builds one with `JsonRpc.Response("2.0",
        result, id).in[Json]`. A fault is not a `Response` but a `JsonRpc.failure` document.
    entry #jsonrpc   the protocol version, `"2.0"`
    entry #result   the method's encoded result
    entry #id   the id of the request answered
  entry .Failure   the `error` member of a JSON-RPC error response: a code, a message and optional data
    detail
        `Failure(code, message, data)` is the object a conformant peer puts in the `error`
        member of a response in place of `result`. The codes are the specification's:
        `-32700` for unparseable JSON, `-32600` for an invalid request, `-32601` for an
        unknown method, `-32602` for invalid parameters, `-32603` for an internal error, and
        a protocol's own codes beyond them. `JsonRpc.failure` builds the whole response document
        around one; `JsonRpc.answer` reads one back from a response and hands it to
        `JsonRpc.receiveFailure`, where `JsonRpc.outcome` turns it into a `JsonRpc.Error(Failed)` carrying
        the code and message.
    entry .decodable   the `JsonRpc.Failure is Json.Decodable` given, in implicit scope
        A pure, throwing derivation anchor, as `JsonRpc.Request.decodable` is, so
        `safely(json.error.as[JsonRpc.Failure])` works at a use site.
    entry #code   the numeric error code
    entry #message   the human-readable error message
    entry #data   any further detail the peer attached, or `Unset`
  entry .failure   a specification-correct error response document, echoing the failing request's id
      `JsonRpc.failure(code, message, id)` is the `Json` of an error response: the fault is
      carried in the `error` member as a `.Failure`, there is no `result` member, and `id`
      echoes the failing request's id — as an explicit JSON `null`, as the specification
      requires, when it is omitted because the request was unparseable and its id unknowable.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      JsonRpc.failure(-32601, t"Method not found", 7.in[Json])   // {"jsonrpc":"2.0","error":{"code":-32601,"message":"Method not found"},"id":7}
      JsonRpc.failure(-32700, t"Parse error")                    // …,"id":null}
      ```
  entry .notification   sends a notification, to a `JsonRpc` channel or by POST to an HTTP URL
      Two overloads, each sending a `Request` with no id and returning an already-offered
      `Promise[Unit]`, since a notification expects no answer.
      `JsonRpc.notification(target: JsonRpc, method, payload)` puts the document on the
      target's outgoing channel. `JsonRpc.notification(url: HttpUrl, method, payload)` POSTs
      it to the URL and discards the response; it needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate` and an
      `Online` in scope, and a connection, HTTP, media-type or truncation failure is ignored,
      as a notification's delivery is not confirmed. The `@rpc` client stubs call these for a
      `Unit` method.
  entry .call   sends a request on a `JsonRpc` channel and returns the `Pending` call to await
      `JsonRpc.call(target, method, payload)` mints a UUID id, registers a promise under it,
      puts the `Request` on the target's outgoing channel, and returns the `.Pending` pair of
      id and promise. `.outcome` awaits it; `.answer` fulfils or cancels it when the response
      arrives. The id is retained because an error response cannot fulfil the promise and
      must be matched back to the caller by id.
  entry .request   sends a request, on a `JsonRpc` channel or by POST to an HTTP URL, as a promise of the result
      Two overloads. `JsonRpc.request(target: JsonRpc, method, payload)` is `.call` without
      the id: the `Promise[Json]` of the result, which `.answer` fulfils when the response
      arrives on the connection's reader, or cancels if the peer answered with an error (the
      fault is then only recoverable through `.outcome` on the `.Pending`).
      `JsonRpc.request(url: HttpUrl, method, payload)` POSTs the `Request` to the URL in a task
      and fulfils the promise with the response document; it needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate` and
      an `Online` in scope, and a connection, HTTP, media-type or parse failure cancels the
      promise, so the awaiting caller sees an `Async.Error`.
  entry .outcome   awaits a `Pending` call's response, raising the peer's fault as a `JsonRpc.Error`
      `JsonRpc.outcome(pending)` awaits the promise, needing a `Monitor` and a
      `Tactic[JsonRpc.Error]`. A response with a `result` is returned as `Json`; one with an
      `error` arrives as `JsonRpc.Error(Failed, code, message)`, with the peer's code and
      message for the caller to map onto its own vocabulary; and a promise cancelled with no
      fault recorded — the connection ended first — is `JsonRpc.Error(Abandoned)`. The
      generated `#proxy` methods call this.
  entry .answer   routes an incoming response to the `.call` awaiting it, by id
      `JsonRpc.answer(json)` reads the response's `id` and exactly one of `result` and
      `error`: a `result` fulfils the pending promise with `.receive`, and an `error` records
      the `.Failure` and cancels it with `.receiveFailure`. A document with no readable `id`,
      or an id nothing is waiting on, is ignored. A `.serve` dispatcher calls this for any
      document without a `method` member, so a connection's reader can feed every incoming
      document to one dispatcher.
  entry .receive   fulfils the pending call of the given id with its result
      `JsonRpc.receive(id, result)` completes and forgets the promise registered under `id`;
      an unknown id is ignored. `.answer` is the usual caller.
  entry .receiveFailure   records a fault for the pending call of the given id and cancels it
      `JsonRpc.receiveFailure(id, failure)` stores the `.Failure` for `.outcome` to find and
      cancels the promise registered under `id`, since an error response carries no result to
      fulfil it with. `.answer` is the usual caller.
  entry .Pending   a request in flight: its correlation id and the promise of its response
    detail
        Returned by `JsonRpc.call` and consumed by `JsonRpc.outcome`. The id is kept beside the promise
        because an error response cannot fulfil the promise and is matched back by id.
    entry #id   the UUID the request was sent under
    entry #promise   the promise `JsonRpc.answer` fulfils with the result, or cancels on a fault
  entry .Error   raised when a JSON-RPC operation fails: error 721
    detail
        Error 721: _the JSON-RPC operation failed because `reason`_. Thrown by a `JsonRpc.serve`
        dispatcher for a method the interface does not declare (`UnknownMethod`), and raised
        by `JsonRpc.outcome` — and so by every generated `#proxy` method — when the peer answered
        with an error (`Failed`, with `#code` and `#detail` carrying the peer's `error.code`
        and `error.message`) or when the request was never answered because its promise was
        cancelled (`Abandoned`). A protocol client recovers its own error vocabulary from the
        code: an LSP client maps `code` onto an `Lsp.Error.Reason`.
    entry .Reason   why the operation failed
        `UnknownMethod` (1), _the method name was not recognised by the dispatcher_; `Failed`
        (2), _the peer answered the request with an error_; `Abandoned` (3), _the request was
        never answered_. The number is the error's detail number.
    entry .communicable   renders each `JsonRpc.Error.Reason` as its message
    entry #reason   why the operation failed
    entry #code   the peer's `error.code`, when the fault came back over the wire; else `Unset`
    entry #detail   the peer's `error.message`, when the fault came back over the wire; else `Unset`
  entry #client   a stub of the `Origin` interface whose calls are sent on this connection
      `proxy[Origin]`: an `Origin^` whose `@rpc` methods each put a `JsonRpc.Request` on this
      instance's outgoing channel. Its result is a capability, since it retains the
      connection.
  entry #proxy   a stub of any `@rpc` interface whose calls are sent on this connection
      `proxy[Interface]` is a macro which builds an object implementing `Interface`: each
      `@rpc` method encodes its parameters as a `params` object (or a `@bare` parameter as the
      whole `params`), puts the request on this instance's channel with `JsonRpc.call`, awaits
      it with `JsonRpc.outcome`, and decodes the result with the type's `Json.Decodable`,
      summoned inside the generated method under the call's own tactic. A `Unit` method sends
      a notification and returns at once. The macro needs a `Monitor` at its expansion site
      and an `Encodable in Json` for every parameter; a peer's error response throws
      `JsonRpc.Error(Failed)` from the call. A protocol whose surface is split across several
      interfaces builds one proxy per interface, all sharing the one outgoing channel.
  entry #put   queues a document on this connection's outgoing channel
      For messages the stubs do not make: a response a protocol answers itself, a failure
      from `JsonRpc.failure`, a server-initiated notification.
  entry #stop   ends the outgoing channel, so `#outgoing` and `#stream` finish once drained
      A reader of the channel completes after what was already put, rather than waiting for
      more. A subclass which already had a member named `stop()` must override it.
  entry #outgoing   the outgoing documents, as a `Chain[Json]` for the transport's writer to drain
      Each accessor drains the shared queue through a fresh single-owner view: use `outgoing`
      or `stream`, not both, on one instance.
  entry #stream   the outgoing documents as `Sse` events, for a `text/event-stream` response
      Each document becomes an `Sse` whose single `data` line is its compact JSON, which
      `Sse.servable` serves as `text/event-stream`: the server-to-client half of MCP's
      streamable HTTP transport. Use `stream` or `outgoing`, not both, on one instance.

entry .remote   `remote[Interface](url)`: a JSON-RPC client stub which POSTs each call to an HTTP URL
    A macro which builds an object implementing `Interface` from its `@rpc` methods, for a
    dispatcher mounted behind an HTTP route. Each method encodes its parameters as a `params`
    object, POSTs a `JsonRpc.Request` to `url` with `JsonRpc.request`, awaits the response and
    decodes the `result` with the result type's `Json.Decodable`; a `Unit` method sends a
    notification with `JsonRpc.notification` and returns once it is posted.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.virtualThreads
    import probates.cancelProbate
    import internetAccess.online

    case class Ping(message: Text)
    case class Pong(message: Text)

    trait Echo:
      @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong

    supervise:
      val echo = remote[Echo](url"https://api.example.com/rpc")
      echo.call(Ping(t"hello"))   // a JSON-RPC request over HTTP, and its typed response
    ```

    The expansion site must have a `Monitor`, a `Probate` and an `Online` in scope — each
    missing one is a compile error — and an `Encodable in Json` for every parameter and a
    `Json.Decodable` for every result. A connection, HTTP or parse failure cancels the
    request's promise, which the generated method throws from the call as an `Async.Error`.
    For a client over a bidirectional channel rather than HTTP, see `JsonRpc#proxy`.

entry .Sse   one server-sent event: its event name, data lines, id and retry interval
  detail
      `Sse(event, data, id, retry)` is one event of a `text/event-stream`: the `event` name
      (`"message"` unless set), the `data` lines, and the optional `id` and `retry` interval
      in milliseconds. Its companion holds everything for reading and writing the stream:
      `.framable` splits a text stream into events at blank lines, `.decodable` parses one
      event's fields, `.encodable` renders one, `.servable` serves a `Chain[Sse]` as a
      streaming HTTP response, and `.jsonEncodable` and `.textEncodable` make an event from a
      JSON document or a line of text. `Sse.Source` is a replayable buffer of events for a
      client which reconnects with its last-seen id.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The text of an event stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val input = t"event: one\nid: 123\ndata: foobar\ndata: baz\n\ndata: hello world\nretry: 54321"
      frame
        label Framed into events at each blank line
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val input = t"event: one\nid: 123\ndata: foobar\ndata: baz\n\ndata: hello world\nretry: 54321"

            Chain(input).frames[Sse].to[List]
            // List(t"event: one\nid: 123\ndata: foobar\ndata: baz", t"data: hello world\nretry: 54321")
      frame
        label Each event decoded into its fields
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val input = t"event: one\nid: 123\ndata: foobar\ndata: baz\n\ndata: hello world\nretry: 54321"

            Chain(input).frames[Sse].map(_.as[Sse]).to[List]
            // List(Sse(t"one", List(t"foobar", t"baz"), t"123"), Sse(t"message", List(t"hello world"), Unset, 54321L))
      ##
      ```

      A `JsonRpc#stream` is a `Chain[Sse]` of a connection's outgoing documents, which is how an
      MCP server streams its notifications to the client; `Sse.servable` is the given which
      lets an HTTP handler return that chain as the response.
  refer obligatory.JsonRpc
  refer obligatory.frames
  entry .servable   serves a `Chain[Sse]` as a streaming `text/event-stream` HTTP response
      The `Chain[Sse] is Servable` given, in implicit scope: an HTTP handler which returns a
      `Chain[Sse]` sends a flowing body of each event's `.encodable` text, UTF-8 encoded, under
      the media type `text/event-stream`, delivering each event as the chain yields it.
  entry .framable   the `Text is Framable by Sse` given: one frame per event, split at blank lines
      In implicit scope; `text.frames[Sse]` yields each event's lines as one `Text`, without
      the blank line which ended it, and a final event with no terminating blank line as it
      stands. It cannot fail. The frames are then decoded with `.decodable`: `frame.as[Sse]`.
  entry .decodable   parses one event's `field: value` lines into an `Sse`; needs a `Tactic[Sse.Error]`
      The `Sse is Decodable in Text` given, in implicit scope, so `text.as[Sse]` reads one
      framed event. Each line is `field: value`, with one optional space after the colon;
      `event` sets the name, each `data` line is appended in order, `id` is kept as text, and
      `retry` is parsed as a `Long`. A line with no colon raises `Sse.Error(MalformedField)`,
      an unknown field name `Sse.Error(UnknownField)`, and a non-numeric `retry`
      `Sse.Error(BadRetryValue)`; every error in a frame is raised, and a frame with any error
      is never delivered as a half-populated event.
  entry .encodable   renders an `Sse` as its wire text: `event:`, `data:`, `id:` and `retry:` lines, then a blank line
      The `Sse is Encodable in Text` given, in implicit scope: `sse.encode` is `event: name`,
      one `data: line` per data line, `id: …` and `retry: …` when set, each ended by `\n`, and
      a final `\n` to end the event. `.servable` writes this for each event it serves.
  entry .jsonEncodable   makes an `Sse` from a `Json` document: a `message` event with the compact JSON as its data
      The `Json is Encodable in Sse` given, in implicit scope: `json.encode` as an `Sse` has
      the event name `message` and one data line of the document's compact rendering, which is
      what `Sse.Source#put(json)` stores and `JsonRpc#stream` produces.
  entry .textEncodable   makes an `Sse` from a `Text`: a `message` event with the text as its one data line
      The `Text is Encodable in Sse` given, in implicit scope, so `Sse.Source#put(t"…")` works.
  entry .Error   raised when a server-sent event is not valid, or a replay exceeds a source's buffer: error 350
    detail
        Error 350: _the server-sent event was not valid because `reason`_. Raised by
        `Sse.decodable` for a malformed line (`MalformedField`), an unknown field name
        (`UnknownField`) or a `retry` which is not an integer (`BadRetryValue`), and by
        `Sse.Source#stream` when the requested replay reaches further back than the source's
        capacity (`CapacityExceeded`).
    entry .Reason   why the event, or the replay, was not valid
        `MalformedField` (1), _a line did not contain the expected `field: value` separator_;
        `UnknownField` (2), _the field name was not one of `event`, `data`, `id`, or `retry`_;
        `BadRetryValue` (3), _the `retry` field value could not be parsed as an integer_;
        `CapacityExceeded` (4), _the requested replay range exceeded the source buffer
        capacity_. The number is the error's detail number.
    entry .communicable   renders each `Sse.Error.Reason` as its message
    entry #reason   why the event, or the replay, was not valid
  entry .Source   a replayable buffer of events, numbered by id, for a client which reconnects
    detail
        `Sse.Source(capacity)` holds the last `capacity` events in a ring and feeds a live
        stream of them. `#put` stamps each event with its sequence number as its `id`, and
        `#stream(start)` is the chain of events after the id `start` — replayed from the ring,
        then live as they are put — which is the reconnection behaviour the protocol calls
        for: a client sends the `Last-Event-ID` it saw, and the source resumes from it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source = Sse.Source(capacity = 128)
        source.put(t"first update")    // id 0
        source.put(t"second update")   // id 1
        source.put(t"third update")    // id 2
        source.stream(start = 0)       // the events with ids 1 and 2, then those put later
        ```

        The source is synchronised, so events may be put from any thread; but each
        `#stream(start)` call with a start replaces the live spool, ending the stream of any
        earlier consumer, so a source feeds one consumer at a time.
    entry #put   stores and publishes an event, stamping it with its sequence number as its id
        `put(value)` takes anything `Encodable in Sse` — a `Text`, a `Json` or an `Sse` — sets
        its `id` to the next sequence number, keeps it in the ring (evicting the oldest once
        `capacity` is exceeded) and sends it to the live stream.
    entry #stream   the events after a last-seen id, replayed from the buffer and then live
        `stream(start)` with a start id replays every buffered event whose id is greater than
        `start`, then continues with each event put afterwards, as a `Chain[Sse]`; it raises
        `Sse.Error(CapacityExceeded)` if more than `capacity` events have been put since
        `start`, since the replay would have a gap. With no start, it is the live events
        alone. Starting a replay ends any earlier consumer's stream.
  entry #event   the event name, `message` unless the stream named one
  entry #data   the data lines, in order; a consumer joins them with newlines
  entry #id   the event id, if the stream set one, which a client sends back as `Last-Event-ID`
  entry #retry   the reconnection interval in milliseconds, if the stream set one

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `JsonRpc.serve`, `JsonRpc.methods`, `JsonRpc#proxy` and `remote`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the JSON-RPC macros have a stable home.
      Each reads an interface's `@rpc` methods (inherited ones included) and generates code
      which `JsonRpc` and `remote` splice.
  entry .methodNames   the macro behind `JsonRpc.methods`: an interface's `@rpc` method names
  entry .dispatcher   the macro behind `JsonRpc.serve`: a match over method names, decoding and encoding each
  entry .remote   the macro behind `remote`: an object implementing the interface over HTTP POSTs
  entry .client   the macro behind `JsonRpc#proxy`: an object implementing the interface over a channel

entry .Grpc   gRPC over HTTP/2: channels, endpoints, the canonical statuses, message framing and derived stubs
  detail
      `Grpc` groups the gRPC vocabulary — `Status`, `Method`, `Metadata`, `Endpoint`,
      `Channel`, `Error` and `Framing` — under one name, as `telekinesis` groups its types
      under `Http` and `Http2`. A `Grpc.Channel` is a single persistent HTTP/2 connection on
      which each call opens one multiplexed stream: the request is one length-prefixed
      Protocol Buffers message, the response is one message (`Channel#unary`) or a lazily
      decoded sequence of them (`Channel#serverStreaming`), and the canonical status arrives in
      the response's `grpc-status` trailer. `Grpc.remote` derives a typed stub for a service
      interface of `@rpc` methods over a channel, so a call is a method call.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import logging.silentLogging

      case class Ping(message: Text)
      case class Pong(message: Text)

      trait Echo:
        @rpc def call(request: Ping): Pong

      supervise:
        val endpoint = Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](50051))
        val channel = Grpc.Channel(Http2.Endpoint(endpoint, t"localhost"))
        val echo = Grpc.remote[Echo](channel, t"echo.Echo")
        echo.call(Ping(t"ping"))   // POST /echo.Echo/call, one message each way
      ```

      Messages are encoded and decoded with `locomotion`'s `Encodable in Protobuf` and
      `Decodable in Protobuf`, derived for case classes. The request is always one message:
      client-streaming and bidirectional calls wait on an `Http2` enhancement. A channel
      retains its connection, which holds the ambient `Monitor`, so a channel is a capability
      and lives inside a `supervise` scope; `Grpc.Endpoint(…).session: channel ?=> …` scopes
      one to a block instead, tearing the connection down when the block ends. A response
      with a non-`Ok` status, a non-200 HTTP status, a truncated body or no message raises
      `Grpc.Error`, whose `status` is the canonical `Grpc.Status`.
  refer obligatory.GrpcSessional
  refer obligatory.rpc
  refer obligatory.Framable
  entry .Status   the seventeen canonical gRPC status codes, carried in the `grpc-status` trailer
    detail
        An enum of the canonical codes, each with its numeric `#code`: `Ok` (0) is success and
        every other is a failure — `Cancelled` (1), `Unknown` (2), `InvalidArgument` (3),
        `DeadlineExceeded` (4), `NotFound` (5), `AlreadyExists` (6), `PermissionDenied` (7),
        `ResourceExhausted` (8), `FailedPrecondition` (9), `Aborted` (10), `OutOfRange` (11),
        `Unimplemented` (12), `Internal` (13), `Unavailable` (14), `DataLoss` (15) and
        `Unauthenticated` (16). A `Grpc.Error` carries the status the server returned, so a
        caller matches on it: `case Grpc.Error(Grpc.Status.NotFound, _) => …`. A
        transport-level failure the channel detects itself is reported as `Internal`.
    entry .of   the status of a numeric code, or `Unset` for an unrecognised one
        `Grpc.Status.of(5)` is `Grpc.Status.NotFound`. A channel reads the `grpc-status`
        trailer with this, treating a missing or unrecognised code as `Unknown`.
    entry .communicable   renders a status as its name, for `Grpc.Error`'s message
    entry #code   the numeric code, `0` to `16`
  entry .Method   a fully-qualified gRPC method: a service name and an rpc name
    detail
        `Grpc.Method(service, rpc)` names a method as the wire addresses it, by the HTTP/2
        `:path` `/<package>.<Service>/<Method>` — `Grpc.Method(t"grpc.health.v1.Health",
        t"Check")` for `/grpc.health.v1.Health/Check`. `Channel#unary` and
        `Channel#serverStreaming` take one; `Grpc.remote` builds one per `@rpc` method from the
        service name and the method's own name.
    entry #service   the fully-qualified service name, the package and the `Service`
    entry #rpc   the method's name within the service
    entry #path   the HTTP/2 `:path`, `/service/rpc`
  entry .Metadata   custom call metadata: key/value pairs sent as HTTP/2 headers with the call
    detail
        `Grpc.Metadata(entries)` is a list of header name and value pairs sent alongside the
        gRPC pseudo-headers — containerd's `containerd-namespace`, an authorisation token. A
        channel's `defaults` are sent with every call, and a call's own metadata is appended
        after them.
    entry #entries   the header name and value pairs, in order
  entry .Endpoint   a gRPC endpoint: the target of a scoped channel, `Grpc.Endpoint(…).session`
    detail
        `Grpc.Endpoint(endpoint, defaults)` pairs an `Http2.Endpoint` — any `Connectable`,
        `Showable` address, such as a TCP `Endpoint` or a Unix socket — with the `Metadata`
        every call should carry. Its only use is as the subject of a session: the companion's
        `.sessional` given makes it `Sessional`, so `Grpc.Endpoint(…).session: channel ?=> …`
        opens the HTTP/2 connection, completes the handshake, lends a `Grpc.Channel^` to the
        block, and tears the connection down when the block ends — where `Grpc.Channel.apply`
        holds its connection open until the enclosing `supervise` scope ends.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import logging.silentLogging

        case class Ping(message: Text)
        case class Pong(message: Text)

        supervise:
          val endpoint = Http2.Endpoint(Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](50051)), t"localhost")

          Grpc.Endpoint(endpoint).session: channel ?=>
            channel.unary[Ping, Pong](Grpc.Method(t"echo.Echo", t"Call"), Ping(t"ping"))
        ```

        The channel is a fresh capability of the block: returning it, or anything which
        captures it, from the block is rejected by the capture checker.
    entry .sessional   the given which makes a `Grpc.Endpoint` the subject of a scoped channel session
        A `GrpcSessional[endpoint]`, in implicit scope, for any `Connectable`, `Showable`
        endpoint type. It needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, a
        `Tactic[Socket.Error]` and a `Socket.Event is Loggable` in scope, and captures them,
        since the connection's reader and writer daemons run under them.
    entry #endpoint   the HTTP/2 endpoint to connect to
    entry #defaults   the metadata sent with every call on the channel
  entry .remote   `Grpc.remote[Interface](channel, service)`: a typed client stub over a channel
      A macro which builds an object implementing `Interface` from its `@rpc` methods, each
      of which must take exactly one request message. A method returning a value makes a
      `Channel#unary` call; one returning a `Chain[Response]` makes a `Channel#serverStreaming`
      call. The `:path` of each is `/service/method`, from the fully-qualified proto service
      name given and the method's own name, so `Grpc.remote[Echo](channel, t"echo.Echo")`
      sends `call` to `/echo.Echo/call`.

      The Protocol Buffers codecs (`Encodable in Protobuf` for each request, `Decodable in
      Protobuf` for each response) and the call's capabilities — a `Monitor` and tactics for
      `Grpc.Error`, `Http2.Error`, `Async.Error` and `Protobuf.Error` — are summoned at the
      `remote` call site, so it must be invoked where they are in scope; the stub's methods
      then have the interface's plain signatures, and a failed call throws through the tactic
      captured there. A method with zero or several parameters is a compile error.
  entry .Channel   a gRPC channel over one persistent HTTP/2 connection, for unary and server-streaming calls
    detail
        A `Channel` wraps an `Http2.Connection`, the `:authority` to send, and the `Metadata`
        every call carries. `Channel.apply(endpoint)` opens one inside a `supervise` scope and
        leaves its connection open until the scope ends; `Grpc.Endpoint(…).session` lends one
        for the duration of a block. Each call is one HTTP/2 stream: a `POST` to the method's
        `:path` with `content-type: application/grpc+proto`, `te: trailers` and the metadata
        as headers, a body of exactly one `Grpc.Framing`-prefixed message, and a response
        whose messages are deframed from the body and whose status is read from the
        `grpc-status` and `grpc-message` fields of the trailers (or of the headers, for a
        Trailers-Only response).

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate

        case class Ping(message: Text)
        case class Pong(message: Text)

        supervise:
          val channel = Grpc.Channel(Http2.Endpoint(Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](50051)), t"localhost"))
          val method = Grpc.Method(t"echo.Echo", t"Call")
          val pong: Pong = channel.unary[Ping, Pong](method, Ping(t"ping"))
          val pongs: Chain[Pong] = channel.serverStreaming[Ping, Pong](method, Ping(t"ping"))
        ```

        A channel is a capability (`Channel^{monitor, caps.any}`), since its connection holds
        the ambient `Monitor`. The constructor `new Channel(connection, authority, defaults)`
        is public, for a connection opened by other means; the `Host` of the requests it
        builds is taken from the part of `authority` before any `:`, or is `Localhost` when
        that is not a hostname (a bracketed IPv6 literal, say), which is harmless as the
        HTTP/2 transport sends `authority` as `:authority` and ignores it.
    entry .apply   opens a channel to a cleartext (h2c) endpoint, completing the HTTP/2 handshake
        `Grpc.Channel(endpoint, defaults)` connects the `Http2.Endpoint`, completes the
        handshake and returns the channel. The connection's read and write daemons capture
        the ambient `Monitor` and `Probate`, so it must be called inside a `supervise` scope,
        and it needs a `Tactic[Async.Error]` and a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` for a connection
        which cannot be made. The connection stays open until the scope ends; for one scoped
        to a block, use `Grpc.Endpoint(…).session`.
    entry #unary   sends one message and reads exactly one response message, then checks the status
        `channel.unary[Request, Response](method, value, metadata)` encodes `value` with its
        `Encodable in Protobuf`, sends it, decodes the first response message with the
        `Decodable in Protobuf` for `Response` and returns it. It needs a `Monitor` and tactics
        for `Grpc.Error`, `Http2.Error`, `Async.Error` and `Protobuf.Error`. The trailing
        status is checked before a message is demanded, so a Trailers-Only error response
        raises `Grpc.Error` with its real status rather than `Internal` for a missing message;
        an HTTP status other than 200, a truncated body, or a successful status with no
        message each raise `Grpc.Error(Internal, …)`.
    entry #serverStreaming   sends one message and lazily decodes each response message as a `Chain`
        `channel.serverStreaming[Request, Response](method, value, metadata)` is as `#unary`,
        but returns the `Chain[Response]` of every message in the response body, decoded as
        the chain is forced. The trailing status is verified only once the body is exhausted,
        so a non-`Ok` status is raised at the chain's end, and the chain must be consumed
        within the `supervise` scope which owns the channel.
  entry .Error   raised when a gRPC call fails: the canonical status and the server's message
    detail
        _the gRPC call failed with status `status`: `detail`_. Raised by `Channel#unary` and
        `Channel#serverStreaming` (and so by every `Grpc.remote` stub method) when the
        response's `grpc-status` is not `Ok`, with `#status` the canonical `Grpc.Status` and
        `#detail` the `grpc-message` text; and with `Grpc.Status.Internal` and a local
        diagnostic when the server returned an HTTP status other than 200, sent no message to
        a unary call, cut the response short, or sent a truncated message frame
        (`Grpc.Framing.framable`). The error carries no fulminate error number.
    entry #status   the canonical status, mirroring the `grpc-status` code
    entry #detail   the `grpc-message` text, or a local diagnostic
  entry .Framing   gRPC's message framing: a compression flag byte, a four-byte length, then the payload
    detail
        Every gRPC message, whatever its codec, is prefixed on the wire by one byte which is
        `1` if the payload is compressed with the call's `grpc-encoding` (gzip here) and `0`
        otherwise, then its length as four big-endian bytes. `.encode` prefixes one message,
        and `.framable` reassembles whole messages from an arbitrarily chunked response body,
        so `body.frames[Grpc.Framing]` is the `Iterator[Data]` of its messages, decompressed
        where flagged. `Grpc.Framing` is the sealed type the convention is named by; its
        failures are `Grpc.Error`, not `Framing.Error`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val framed: Data = Grpc.Framing.encode(t"hello".in[Data])   // 00 00 00 00 05, then the five bytes
        Chain(framed).frames[Grpc.Framing].to[List].map(_.utf8)     // List(t"hello")

        Chain(Grpc.Framing.encode(t"compress me please".in[Data], compress = true))
        . frames[Grpc.Framing].to[List].map(_.utf8)                 // List(t"compress me please")
        ```
    entry .encode   prefixes one message for the wire, gzip-compressing it if asked
        `Grpc.Framing.encode(message, compress)` is the five-byte header followed by the
        payload: the message as given, or its gzip compression with the flag byte set when
        `compress` is `true` (it is `false` unless set). The channel sends every request
        message uncompressed.
    entry .framable   the `Data is Framable by Grpc.Framing` given; needs a `Tactic[Grpc.Error]`
        In implicit scope. Reads each five-byte header and takes that many bytes, gunzipping
        a payload whose flag is set; the input ending at a message boundary ends the
        iterator, and ending inside a header or a payload raises `Grpc.Error(Internal,
        "the gRPC message frame was truncated")`. Lengths are masked byte by byte, so a
        message of 128 bytes or more is framed correctly.

entry .GrpcSessional   the `Sessional` instance which lends a scoped `Grpc.Channel` for a `Grpc.Endpoint`
  detail
      `GrpcSessional[endpoint]` is the class behind `Grpc.Endpoint.sessional`: its `Self` is
      `Grpc.Endpoint[endpoint]` and its `Result` is a fresh `Grpc.Channel^`. `session(target)`
      opens the endpoint's HTTP/2 connection through `Http2.EndpointSessional`, completes the
      handshake, lends a channel over it to the block, and tears the connection and its
      reader and writer daemons down when the block ends — unlike `Grpc.Channel.apply`,
      whose connection a parked daemon holds open until the enclosing `supervise` scope ends.
      It is a named class rather than an anonymous given because an anonymous instance would
      freshen the capability types in its inferred `Result`.

      It is constructed with a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, a
      `Tactic[Socket.Error]` and a `Socket.Event is Loggable`, all of which
      `Grpc.Endpoint.sessional` takes from scope; a user writes
      `Grpc.Endpoint(endpoint).session: channel ?=> …` and never names the class.
  refer obligatory.Grpc
  entry #Self   the subject type, `Grpc.Endpoint[endpoint]`
  entry #Result   the lent value, a fresh `Grpc.Channel^` which may not escape the block
  entry #session   opens the connection, lends a channel to the block, and closes it after
      `session(target)(lambda)`: the block receives the `Grpc.Channel^` as a contextual
      value, so a call inside it is `channel.unary[…](…)` with `channel` the context parameter
      the block names. A connection which cannot be made raises `Socket.Error` or
      `Async.Error` through the tactics the instance captured.

entry .grpcInternal   the macro implementation behind `Grpc.remote`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the gRPC stub macro has a stable home.
  entry .remote   the macro behind `Grpc.remote`: an object implementing the interface over a channel
      For each `@rpc` method of the interface, generates a body which summons the Protocol
      Buffers codecs, the `Monitor` and the four tactics at the expansion site and calls
      `Grpc.Channel#unary` or, for a `Chain` result, `Grpc.Channel#serverStreaming`.
